## Implementing an Esolang
<hr style="width:100%;border-bottom:3px solid black;">

!!! tip "Tip"
    This demo builds on several others: an awk [polyglot]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/demos/polyglots) for the lexer, the [`io.stack.*` structured stack]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/standard-lib) for state, and the idea that [everything is a target]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/language/#homoiconic), so a decoded program is just a stream of `make` goals.

Underload[^1] is a small, stack-based, concatenative esoteric language: a program is a string of single-character commands operating on a stack of strings.  The full command set:

| Operator | Operation | Effect |
| :-: | --- | --- |
| `(`...`)` | push | push the enclosed text onto the stack as a quotation |
| `~` | swap | `(x)(y)` -> `(y)(x)` |
| `:` | dup | `(x)` -> `(x)(x)` |
| `!` | discard | `(x)` -> (nothing) |
| `*` | concatenate | `(x)(y)` -> `(xy)` |
| `a` | enclose | `(x)` -> `((x))` |
| `S` | print | pop `(x)` and output `x` |
| `^` | apply | pop `(x)` and *run `x` as Underload* |

That last command (`^`) is what makes Underload Turing-complete, and makes quines delightfully easy.

------------------

This demo implements a complete Underload interpreter with `compose.mk`, and we'll see how easy the pieces map cleanly onto the standard library.  

* The **lexer** is an awk define that rewrites program text into one dispatchable goal per line
* The **stack** is `io.stack.*`
* Each **operator** is a target
* The decoded program is run by `mk.kernel.each`, which dispatches each line as an instruction (order and repetition preserved).  

Bundled programs include a quine, a palindromic quine, and an (abstracted) factorial.

```bash {.cli_example}
# Run the bundled demo programs:
$ ./demos/underload.mk

# Evaluate a program from stdin:
$ printf '(Hello, world!)S' | ./demos/underload.mk ul.eval

# A quine, which prints its own source:
$ printf '(a(:^)*S):^' | ./demos/underload.mk ul.eval
```

